<Layout
	title="Executive dashboard for a consumer goods company"
	description="Pulse: one page that tells a consumer goods leadership team what is off, why, and what to do next."
	pubDate={new Date('October 17 2026')}
	updatedDate={new Date('October 19 2026')}
>
	<p><em>Prototype. Desktop only for now. Northstar Consumer Goods is fictional and all data is synthetic. Every link below opens the dashboard at the exact place it describes.</em></p>

	<p>
		<a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Open the dashboard →</strong></a> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/metric-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>See how the metrics fit together →</strong></a>
	</p>

	<h2>Problem</h2>
	<p>
		Design a single-page executive dashboard for a consumer goods (CPG) company that helps the C-suite understand
		performance, diagnose what is behind it and decide what to do. It must be interactive, <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CEO/filters" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cut by geography, channel, category and brand</a>,
		and combine internal and external data.
	</p>

	<h2>Users and their jobs</h2>
	<p>
		Five roles: <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CEO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CEO</a>, <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CFO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CFO</a> (including revenue growth management), <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CMO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMO</a> (including brand),
		<a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CSO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chief Sales Officer</a> and <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#COO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">COO</a> (including manufacturing and procurement). Legal, tech and HR are out of scope.
		Each has three jobs:
	</p>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>What is the data?</strong> The few numbers they run, against plan, latest estimate and last year.</li>
		<li><strong>What does it mean?</strong> What is off, by how much, against what goal, and why.</li>
		<li><strong>What do I do?</strong> The next action, who owns it, by when, and follow-up until it is closed.</li>
	</ol>

	<h2>1. What is the data: a metric pyramid</h2>
	<p>
		An executive's time is short, so each role gets a fast, broad view and an easy way down into any detail. Each role
		has <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CEO/metrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">four core metrics</a>, chosen from what CPG boards measure and pay executives on: growth, margin,
		cash and share. Each metric breaks into the parts that add up to it, in its own unit, and then into the levers that
		move it, each with one owner. Lenses such as category growth sit beside a metric, never inside its sum. Because every
		breakdown reconciles, a CFO can walk <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#v=eyJyb2xlIjoiQ0ZPIiwiY2FyZCI6IkdNIiwicGYiOnt9LCJwZXJpb2QiOiJZVEQiLCJjb21wIjoiVCIsInllYXIiOiJDQUwiLCJwYXRoIjpbeyJmbiI6Im9wZW5Ob2RlIiwiYXJncyI6WyJDT0dTIl19XSwiY2hhcnQiOm51bGx9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">gross margin from 52.2% plan to 51.2%</a> and see that cost per case takes
		78 bps off. The full tree, with formulas, owners and sources, is in the <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/metric-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">metric pyramid</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		Data comes from three places: market and shopper data, internal actuals, and forward-looking plans and targets from
		planning and revenue growth management tools. Named integrations in the dashboard are illustrative.
	</p>

	<h2>2. What does it mean: the reason, not just the number</h2>
	<p>
		Every card and <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CEO/summary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">issue in the summary</a> reads in the order an executive thinks: what is off, by how
		much, against what goal, the value now, and why. For example, <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#v=eyJyb2xlIjoiQ0VPIiwiY2FyZCI6Ik9TRyIsInBmIjp7fSwicGVyaW9kIjoiWVREIiwiY29tcCI6IlQiLCJ5ZWFyIjoiQ0FMIiwicGF0aCI6W3siZm4iOiJvcGVuTm9kZSIsImFyZ3MiOlsiUE1YIl19XSwiY2hhcnQiOm51bGx9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">organic sales growth is 0.4 pts behind plan</a>:
		price/mix takes 0.7 pts off, because price increases landed at +2.9% against +3.3% planned and the oats increase is
		stuck at UK and US retailers. <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CFO-GM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Any number opens a deep dive in place</a>, showing its parts, where it is
		off and the actions on it. Hover over any metric name for what it is, how to read it, its owner and its formula.
	</p>
	<p>
		An AI layer writes the summary and <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CMO/ask" target="_blank" rel="noopener">turns a plain-English question into a custom view</a> with its own
		filters. In production this would be an agent. Here it is simulated with rules on synthetic data.
	</p>

	<h2>3. What do I do: actions with owners</h2>
	<p>
		Each issue carries a suggested next step, the role that owns it and a due date. Users assign it from any level,
		email the owner or book a meeting, and <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#COO/actions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">track it to done</a>, with overdue items flagged. For example,
		<a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#v=eyJyb2xlIjoiQ09PIiwiY2FyZCI6IlBPUiIsInBmIjp7ImMiOlsiSUQiXX0sInBlcmlvZCI6IllURCIsImNvbXAiOiJUIiwieWVhciI6IkNBTCIsInBhdGgiOltdLCJjaGFydCI6bnVsbH0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">perfect order in Indonesia</a> is 11.5 pts behind target, and the open action is for the Indonesia Supply Lead to
		rush the inbound shipments and get fill rate back to 95% within 3 weeks. Tools sit inside the flow where the decision is
		made, so a Chief Sales Officer who is <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#v=eyJyb2xlIjoiQ1NPIiwiY2FyZCI6IlNBTEVTIiwicGYiOnt9LCJwZXJpb2QiOiJZVEQiLCJjb21wIjoiVCIsInllYXIiOiJDQUwiLCJwYXRoIjpbXSwiY2hhcnQiOm51bGx9" target="_blank" rel="noopener">$1.9M behind the October sales target</a> can re-check the promotion plan
		from the same view.
	</p>

	<h2>What brings them back</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong><a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CEO/watchlist" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watchlist</a>:</strong> bookmark any view or chart and see what changed since you saved it, with an optional Teams alert when it crosses a value or range you set.</li>
		<li><strong><a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#CMO/ask" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ask</a>:</strong> type a question and get a custom view you can save or share.</li>
		<li><strong>Shareable views:</strong> every view has its own link, so a number can be shared exactly as seen. Each link on this page is one.</li>
		<li><strong>Roadmap:</strong> mobile view, product-level data, and sign-in with watchlists stored per user.</li>
	</ul>

	<p>
		<strong>Try it in a minute:</strong> <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">pick a role</a>, open the reddest card, follow the breakdown to the cause and
		assign the fix. Or <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/#demo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">run the guided demo</a>.
	</p>

	<p>
		<a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>Open the dashboard →</strong></a> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <a href="/experiments/executive-pulse/metric-pyramid.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong>See how the metrics fit together →</strong></a>
	</p>
</Layout>
